{
  "about": "Store facts and what they mean, read by every side of the billing contract (3.0). Each platform turns its own store facts into the same answer shape (src/pro/schema.ts StoreAnswer); the app then decides the entitlement from the answer alone (src/pro/entitlement.ts). Swift maps ios.facts to ios.answer (ios/ZeroBilling); Kotlin maps android.facts to android.answer (android/app/src/play, PlayAnswer). The JS test checks each answer is well-formed and reduces, from a phone that never had Pro, to `entitlement`, and from a phone that had Pro to `whenProBefore`: `lifetime` is a phone showing the lifetime purchase, `renewingMonthly` one showing a renewing monthly subscription. On iOS that subscription's stored period ended a day before `nowMs` (so the offline allowance is in play); on Android no end date is ever stored, because Google Play's client reports none. The two stores differ where a scenario says so: on the App Store every ending leaves a transaction, so an empty store cannot say; on Google Play a purchase that ended is simply no longer returned, so an empty answer is an answer.",
